merge("entities/charles-breyer.md", u"""
## 2025 National Guard rulings

Breyer, sitting in San Francisco, ruled in June 2025 that President [[donald-trump|Donald Trump]]'s federalization of roughly 4,000 [[california-army-national-guard|California National Guard]] troops for Los Angeles violated federal law — 10 U.S.C. § 12406, which authorizes such deployments only for invasion, rebellion, or inability to enforce the laws — as well as the Tenth Amendment's protection of state sovereignty, and he ordered control returned to Governor [[gavin-newsom|Gavin Newsom]].[{n}] The [[united-states-court-of-appeals-for-the-ninth-circuit|Ninth Circuit]] stayed his June 12 order almost immediately, and on June 19 a unanimous three-judge panel held that Trump likely had statutory authority to federalize the Guard, finding that the Los Angeles protests met the "danger of rebellion" threshold.[{n}]

On September 2, 2025, Breyer issued a further ruling enjoining the administration from using the remaining federalized California Guard members, or any military force, for domestic law enforcement in California, citing the [[posse-comitatus-act|Posse Comitatus Act]].[{n}] The two orders left the administration holding federalized California troops it could not deploy within California, a situation that preceded the [[2025-portland-national-guard-deployment|2025 Portland National Guard deployment]].[{n}]
""")

